  "summary": "Lynn, MA police credit Flock license plate readers with helping track a stolen car containing a 6-year-old, which Everett officers stopped 39 minutes later; the child was unharmed and a suspect was arraigned. The article contextualizes the incident with Massachusetts case law (Commonwealth v. McCarthy) and notes ACLU litigation and several Massachusetts municipalities that have barred local ALPR use.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The department credits the Flock license plate reader network with showing that the car had left Lynn toward Saugus.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "The court said widespread use could implicate a protected expectation of privacy in the whole of a person's public movements, and it noted that the exigency exception could apply to real-time hot-list alerts.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "The page also lists Cambridge, Framingham, Greenfield, Natick and Watertown as municipalities that have barred their local police from using plate readers.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "What the release does not say is how many cameras were involved, how long Lynn keeps the data, or who else can search it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    }
  ],
